Bronchiectasis
Respiratory System
Copious purulent sputum, Coarse basilar crackles, Signet-ring sign on HRCT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-009
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic cough with increased volume and purulence of sputum, associated with pleuritic pain and low-grade fever.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with known chronic lung disease experiencing a sudden increase in foul-smelling purulent sputum, hemoptysis, and chest pain. Urgent assessment was required to initiate targeted antibiotic therapy and rule out massive hemoptysis or severe lobar pneumonia before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am coughing up half a cup of green, foul-tasting phlegm every morning and my lower chest aches when I breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic, daily cough productive of copious sputum for years. Over the last 4 days, the volume has doubled, becoming foul-smelling and dark green. They report intermittent mild hemoptysis (blood streaking) over the past 24 hours. Pleuritic chest pain has developed during this exacerbation, localized to the bilateral lower ribs. The patient notes worsening progressive exertional dyspnea, overwhelming fatigue, and a 2kg weight loss over the past month due to the high metabolic demand of recurrent respiratory infections.
Functional Status: Independent but currently limiting all physical activity due to fatigue and dyspnea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of exhaustion from continuous coughing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/78 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.9 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Chronically ill appearance, audible rattling respirations, halitosis present.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals prominent coarse crackles and low-pitched wheezing predominantly at the lung bases bilaterally, which alter slightly after coughing. Digital clubbing is distinct on bilateral hands. Prior medical records note HRCT findings of dilated, thickened airways ("tram-track" lines) and the signet-ring sign.
Environmental Observations: Sputum mug at bedside containing significant volume of purulent material.
Medication Reconciliation: On regular mucolytics; last course of antibiotics was 6 weeks ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bronchiectasis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Lung Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral basal distribution of crackles rather than a single focal cavity, and absence of swinging high fevers.

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Purulent sputum production, fever, and chronicity of underlying symptoms preceding the acute pain.

Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Basal predominance (TB favors apices) and known structural bronchiectasis history, though must remain a consideration if unresponsive to standard therapy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute infective exacerbation of known bronchiectasis, characterized by a significant increase in copious purulent sputum, fever, new pleuritic pain, and minor hemoptysis. Coarse basilar crackles confirm significant secretion retention in the dilated airways. The development of fever and hemoptysis marks a destabilization of their chronic condition. Delaying clinical evaluation and antibiotic initiation until the next in-hours period would allow the endobronchial infection to worsen, potentially progressing to destructive pneumonia or massive hemoptysis requiring ICU admission. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to prescribe targeted, high-dose antibiotics and enforce aggressive airway clearance techniques.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg twice daily for 14 days based on previous sputum culture sensitivities. Advised strict rest and increased oral hydration.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform active cycle of breathing techniques (ACBT) accompanied by postural drainage twice daily; executing this physical routine actively forces pooled secretions out of the damaged lower airways, which directly stops bacterial overgrowth and reduces infection frequency.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to respiratory physiotherapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Flutter valve / Acapella device recommended for secretion clearance.
Follow-up: GP review in 3 days; provide an early morning sputum sample for culture and sensitivity prior to starting antibiotics if possible.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if coughing produces more than a tablespoon of fresh red blood, if sharp chest pain becomes unbearable, or if confusion occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of exacerbation is clinical; efficacy of antibiotics depends on exact bacterial sensitivities which may require adjustment.
Clinical Course: Expect a gradual reduction in sputum volume and purulence over 5 to 7 days; fatigue may persist for several weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you cough up frank blood, experience severe shortness of breath at rest, or develop a high fever over 39 C.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your damaged airways trap mucus, creating a breeding ground for bacteria; the 14-day course of antibiotics is required to fully eradicate the deep-seated infection.
Actionable Steps: Drink at least 2.5 liters of water daily; this specific hydration target thins out the thick mucus in your chest, making it significantly easier to cough up and clear the infection.

